  "text": "We are given that $ P(x) $ is a **cubic polynomial** with **real coefficients**, **positive roots**, one of which is 3, and the **roots are in arithmetic progression**. Also, the **coefficients of $ P(x) $ are integers**. We are to find how many such polynomials exist.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: General Form of a Cubic Polynomial\n\nA cubic polynomial with roots $ r_1, r_2, r_3 $ can be written as:\n\n$$\nP(x) = a(x - r_1)(x - r_2)(x - r_3)\n$$\n\nGiven that the roots are in **arithmetic progression**, we can write them as:\n\n$$\nr_1 = a - d, \\quad r_2 = a, \\quad r_3 = a + d\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $ a - d, a, a + d $, forming an arithmetic progression with common difference $ d $.\n\nSince the polynomial has **positive roots**, all three of $ a - d, a, a + d $ must be **positive**. So:\n\n- $ a - d > 0 \\Rightarrow a > d $\n- $ a + d > 0 $, which is always true if $ a > 0 $ and $ d > 0 $\n- Also, $ a > 0 $\n\nSo $ a > d > 0 $\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: One of the Roots is 3\n\nOne of the roots is 3. Since the roots are in arithmetic progression, the middle root (i.e., $ a $) is the average of the three roots. So if one of the roots is 3, then 3 must be either the middle root or one of the other two.\n\nLet’s suppose that $ a = 3 $. Then the roots are:\n\n$$\n3 - d, \\quad 3, \\quad 3 + d\n$$\n\nThese are three roots in arithmetic progression, with one of them being 3.\n\nSo the roots are: $ 3 - d, 3, 3 + d $\n\nThis is one possible configuration.\n\nNow, since the roots are all positive, we must have:\n\n- $ 3 - d > 0 \\Rightarrow d < 3 $\n- $ 3 + d > 0 $, which is always true if $ d > 0 $\n\nSo $ 0 < d < 3 $\n\nAlso, since the coefficients of $ P(x) $ are integers, the polynomial must have integer coefficients. Since the roots are $ 3 - d, 3, 3 + d $, and the polynomial is:\n\n$$\nP(x) = a(x - (3 - d))(",
